    "explanationEn": "Analysis of public issues usually runs on three levels: description of what is happening, evaluation of who gains and loses and how strong each case is, and solution of how to respond. Proposing compensation belongs to the third: it accepts that the conflict is real and cannot be wholly removed, and turns to how the cost should be shared. A good answer at this level presses further — who pays, how delivery is assured, and whether money can replace non-monetary losses such as community networks and settled ways of living. One option understates what the solution level is; the third files it under data computation; another option abandons the analyst's role, whereas civic education asks precisely that students be able to propose and test solutions."
