    "correctIndex": 0,
    "marks": 1,
    "contentEn": "A line chart's vertical axis begins at 95 rather than 0. What effect does this have?",
    "optionsEn": [
      "small changes are visually magnified and readers tend to overstate the movement",
      "small changes are visually compressed and readers tend to understate the movement",
      "it makes no difference, since the values themselves are labelled",
      "the chart is void, as no chart may begin anywhere but zero"
    ],
    "explanationEn": "Truncating the axis compresses the range of values into the height of the chart, so a rise from 96 to 98 may occupy half the plot and read as a surge. The values are labelled, but readers take in the SHAPE before the numbers, which is precisely why the device works. Note that truncation is not in itself dishonest — where values cluster in a narrow band, as with body temperature or unemployment, starting at zero would render the movement invisible. The issue is whether the reader notices, which is why the first step in reading any chart is to check where the vertical axis begins. One option pushes \"be alert\" into \"never permissible\", an over-inference."
